---
title: Specimen Parts
sidebar:
  label: Specimen parts
  order: 6
---

Use `Specimen Parts` for voucher preparations, tissues, swabs, and other
physical parts derived from the specimen. Each specimen can have multiple
parts. Organisms found living on or in the specimen are recorded separately as
[symbionts](#symbionts).

The `Specimen Parts` card has icon tabs: the first holds parts, the bug icon
holds symbionts, and the storage icon holds
[associated data](/en/usages/specimens/associated-data-and-media/). The
symbiont tab is available for mammals, birds, herpetofauna, and fishes, but not
for invertebrate catalogs. For a fish lot with recorded
individuals, parts and symbionts can be linked to the individuals they came
from; see [Fishes](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/fishes/#parts-and-symbionts).

Part fields include:

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Tissue ID` | Optional. While the field is empty, the repeat icon copies the last tissue ID entered for this specimen. `Tissue ID options` offers `New number`, which takes the next configured tissue number, and `Settings`. |
| `Individuals` | Only for a fish lot with recorded individuals. Links the part to individuals in the lot. |
| `QR/barcode ID` | Optional unique ID for a tube, tag, or container. Scan it with a camera, or generate a UUID. |
| `Preparation type` | From your specimen-type list. |
| `Counts` | Number of parts or containers. |
| `Treatment`, `Additional treatment` | Preservation treatments. |
| `Time taken` | When the part was taken; recommended for fresh tissues. |
| `Remarks` | Notes about this part. |

These fields appear after `Show more`, or once they hold a value:

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| `Preparator` | When different from the voucher's preparator. |
| `Date taken` | When the part was taken. |
| `PMI` | Post-mortem interval, such as `1:30`. |
| `Storage type`, `Storage location` | Storage medium, and the freezer, cabinet, shelf, or box position. |
| `Museum permanent`, `Museum loan` | The museum holding the part, and one receiving it on loan. |

Custom fields for parts appear in the form too.

The part list shows the part type, count, barcode ID, preparator, tissue ID,
treatments, date and time, PMI, linked fish individuals, and whether a remark
exists.

## Symbionts

A symbiont is any organism living on or in the host specimen, whatever its
relationship to the host: parasitic, mutualistic, commensal, phoretic, and so
on. Two fields describe the association:

- `Interaction type`: how the symbiont relates to its host. The default
  vocabulary is `Parasitic`, `Parasitoid`, `Mutualistic`, `Commensal`,
  `Phoretic`, and `Unknown`. Records created before NAHPU tracked symbionts
  were migrated with the interaction type `Parasitic`.
- `Category`: where the symbiont lives on its host. The defaults are
  `Ectosymbiont`, `Endosymbiont`, and `Mesosymbiont`.

On the specimen itself, `Symbionts examined` and `Symbionts detected` record
whether the host was checked and whether anything was found, so an examination
with no symbionts is still documented.

Symbiont records separate collection from identification:

- `Collector`: who recovered the symbiont. It starts as the host's preparator,
  or its cataloger when no preparator is set.
- `Determiner`, `Date determined`, and `Determination method`: who identified
  the symbiont, when, and how. The method uses the same list as the specimen
  identification method.
- `Category` and `Anatomical location`: choosing a category narrows the
  location list to sites that fit it. Older category values, such as
  `Ectoparasite` and `Endoparasite`, narrow it the same way. Locations you added
  yourself always stay in the list.

Life stage, detection method, preparation method, and symbiont ID numbering are
set up in `Settings` > `Specimens`.

Symbiont curation records carry their own `Storage` and `Storage location`
fields so the preservation method and physical location can be recorded
separately.

In Darwin Core exports, the interaction type becomes
`organismInteractionType`. A record without an interaction type falls back to
its category. When an interaction type is set, the category and association
status go into `organismInteractionDescription`.
